One good solution is to “clamp” the thumbs so that the start value is never allowed to catch up with (or go past) the end value. The idea is to detect when a drag would cause the two thumbs to overlap (or be closer than your minimum gap) and then force one of them to stop at least one step away. For example, if you want a gap of one step (your stepSize), you can immediately correct the value as soon as an overlap is detected.

Below is one way to modify your LaunchedEffect so that whenever the thumbs are moved, you adjust the values if they’re about to cross. (In this example I assume that if they “collide” the thumb being dragged will stop at a minimum distance defined by stepSize.) You may need to experiment with which thumb to “move” (or which value to clamp) if you’d like slightly different behavior.

────────────────────────────
Suggested Code Change:

LaunchedEffect(rangeSliderState) {
    snapshotFlow { 
        rangeSliderState.value.activeRangeStart to rangeSliderState.value.activeRangeEnd 
    }.collect { (start, end) ->
        // First, round the current positions to the nearest step:
        val startRounded = (start / stepSize).roundToInt() * stepSize
        val endRounded = (end / stepSize).roundToInt() * stepSize

        // Then, enforce a minimum gap (one stepSize) so they don’t overlap.
        // You can choose which thumb to clamp. Here I’m forcing the drag so that
        // the start value is never more than (end - stepSize) and similarly,
        // the end value is never less than (start + stepSize).
        val clampedStart = startRounded.coerceAtMost(endRounded - stepSize)
        val clampedEnd = endRounded.coerceAtLeast(startRounded + stepSize)

        // Optional: If the adjustment was needed, update the slider’s state;
        // that will cause the UI to reflect the change immediately.
        if (clampedStart != start || clampedEnd != end) {
            rangeSliderState.value = rangeSliderState.value.copy(
                activeRangeStart = clampedStart,
                activeRangeEnd = clampedEnd
            )
        }

        // Finally, report the validated range.
        onValuesChange(clampedStart..clampedEnd)
    }
}
────────────────────────────

A few notes on this approach:

1. The call to .coerceAtMost(endRounded - stepSize) makes sure the start thumb never catches up to the end thumb and vice‐versa (with a gap equal to stepSize). You can adjust the gap by replacing stepSize with a different minimum gap if desired.

2. By updating the slider state inside the collector (if a change was needed) you help keep the UI consistent with the logic.

3. In some cases you might want to detect which thumb is being dragged (for example by inspecting the interaction sources) and choose to “fix” that one instead of the other. The above approach always clamps the start value when necessary; tweak it if you prefer other behavior.

With these changes the slider “disables” overlapping values by ensuring as soon as a thumb is dragged too far (or collides with the other thumb), it is clamped to a value that maintains the gap. This should avoid the case where the slider gets stuck with both thumbs overlapping.

