The problem is that by immediately calling printWindow.close() after printWindow.print(), you are closing the window before the print dialog has a chance to process the document. To fix it, you need to wait until the printing is finished or the print dialog has been opened before closing the window.

Here are a few approaches you can use:

1. Use a Timeout:
You can delay the closing of the window with setTimeout so that it isn’t closed immediately. For example:

  printWindow.document.open();
  printWindow.document.write("toto");
  printWindow.document.close();
  printWindow.focus();
  printWindow.print();
  setTimeout(function () {
    printWindow.close();
  }, 1000); // Adjust the timing as needed

2. Use the onafterprint Event:
Modern browsers support the onafterprint event, which triggers after the print dialog has been closed. For example:

  printWindow.document.open();
  printWindow.document.write("toto");
  printWindow.document.close();
  printWindow.focus();
  printWindow.print();
  printWindow.onafterprint = function() {
    printWindow.close();
  };

Both methods ensure that the window remains open long enough for the print process to initiate properly.

Note:
• Make sure you remove the 'text/plain' parameter from document.open() (unless you have a specific reason to keep it) because by default you might want to treat it as HTML if you're writing HTML content.
